WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.172
This is the technical deep
dive for Book 03 of the

3
00:00:04.172 --> 00:00:07.924
Federal Modernization
Workbook, HR-Driven Identity.

4
00:00:07.924 --> 00:00:10.637
It's for the people
who'll actually build it:

5
00:00:10.637 --> 00:00:14.975
identity engineers, HR systems
integrators and ICAM architects.

6
00:00:14.975 --> 00:00:19.400
The short briefing makes the case for
letting the personnel record drive identity.

7
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.300
This session goes underneath it.

8
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:26.212
Part 1 is the data and the pipe: who
owns each fact, how a person is keyed,

9
00:00:26.212 --> 00:00:31.650
and how records move from the new
government-wide HR platform into Entra ID.

10
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:36.725
Part 2 is lifecycle, access and evidence,
and the plan that ties it together.

11
00:00:36.725 --> 00:00:39.880
Let's start with why the
data matters so much now.

12
00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:48.095
Here's the problem in one sentence:
a token can prove who signed a claim,

13
00:00:48.095 --> 00:00:50.367
but not that the claim is still true.

14
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:53.255
The timeline shows how an
orphaned account happens.

15
00:00:53.255 --> 00:00:57.739
HR knows about a separation two weeks
early, and nothing else is listening,

16
00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:02.443
so the last forgotten account turns up
in a penetration test six months later.

17
00:01:02.443 --> 00:01:05.618
Nobody was careless;
there was simply no wire.

18
00:01:05.618 --> 00:01:07.106
And the cost has gone up.

19
00:01:07.106 --> 00:01:12.719
Under Zero Trust, access is decided per
session from attributes and dynamic groups,

20
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:16.218
so a wrong department isn't
a phone-book typo any more.

21
00:01:16.218 --> 00:01:17.518
It's wrong access.

22
00:01:17.518 --> 00:01:20.890
Next, the architecture
that fixes the wiring.

23
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.840
The architecture is a straight
line with a stable middle.

24
00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.139
The HR source on the left can change,

25
00:01:30.139 --> 00:01:34.263
and so can the Microsoft products
on the right, but the governed

26
00:01:34.263 --> 00:01:38.703
identity model in the center, one
schema and one person key, stays put.

27
00:01:38.703 --> 00:01:41.292
That's what lets an
agency survive a vendor

28
00:01:41.292 --> 00:01:44.028
change without rewriting
its access policy.

29
00:01:44.028 --> 00:01:48.331
The boundaries matter just as
much: HR owns employment facts,

30
00:01:48.331 --> 00:01:53.753
Entra ID owns credentials and sessions,
and the card system owns the PIV card.

31
00:01:53.753 --> 00:01:57.378
No system becomes authoritative
just because it holds a copy.

32
00:01:57.378 --> 00:02:00.803
The principles on the right
come back in every module.

33
00:02:00.803 --> 00:02:03.190
So let's start with the data itself.

34
00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:12.610
Module 1 is attribute authority and the
canonical schema: deciding, in writing,

35
00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.616
which system owns each fact,

36
00:02:14.616 --> 00:02:18.803
which key identifies a person,
and where every value lands.

37
00:02:18.803 --> 00:02:22.003
It's the work estates skip
because users never see it,

38
00:02:22.003 --> 00:02:27.420
and it's the work that decides whether
automation spreads truth or spreads errors.

39
00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:33.175
The first misreading to clear up:

40
00:02:33.175 --> 00:02:37.332
making HR the source doesn't make it
a rival to the identity platform.

41
00:02:37.332 --> 00:02:39.145
They own different kinds of fact.

42
00:02:39.145 --> 00:02:42.095
HR owns who a worker
is and where they sit.

43
00:02:42.095 --> 00:02:46.720
Entra ID owns how they prove it and
whether a request is allowed right now.

44
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.959
What makes HR special is effective
dating: it knows about a hire or

45
00:02:50.959 --> 00:02:55.120
a separation before it's true,
along with the date it becomes true.

46
00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:59.120
That's what makes day-one readiness
and same-day revocation possible.

47
00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.442
And the one rule with no exceptions:

48
00:03:01.442 --> 00:03:05.045
fix a wrong value upstream,
never in the directory copy.

49
00:03:05.045 --> 00:03:07.710
Next, how one person is keyed.

50
00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:16.260
One person has several identifiers,
because each layer has its own lifecycle.

51
00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:20.110
The HR system's number changes
if the HR system changes.

52
00:03:20.110 --> 00:03:23.197
The account ID changes if
the account is rebuilt.

53
00:03:23.197 --> 00:03:26.360
A card's identifiers
change on every reissue.

54
00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.857
So the agency needs one enterprise
person key it controls,

55
00:03:29.857 --> 00:03:31.897
with everything else mapped to it.

56
00:03:31.897 --> 00:03:35.206
That's a real decision,
made before Phase 1 closes,

57
00:03:35.206 --> 00:03:40.748
because the move to the new HR platform is
exactly the moment a source key changes.

58
00:03:40.748 --> 00:03:44.047
Get it right and a rehire
re-links to the same person,

59
00:03:44.047 --> 00:03:48.447
a contractor who converts keeps
their key, and audit joins just work.

60
00:03:48.447 --> 00:03:50.880
Now, where the values come from.

61
00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:59.280
For federal employees, the real
contract isn't Oracle's field names.

62
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.658
It's OPM's standard personnel data: the
same elements and codes in every agency,

63
00:04:04.658 --> 00:04:08.355
and a nature-of-action code
on every personnel action.

64
00:04:08.355 --> 00:04:13.343
Mapping identity to the standard means
one crosswalk works for every employee

65
00:04:13.343 --> 00:04:18.255
population, survives the next vendor
change, and can be reused across agencies.

66
00:04:18.255 --> 00:04:22.900
OPM requires the coded blocks of the
SF-50 to use only standard codes,

67
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:26.443
which is why they're dependable
enough to drive access.

68
00:04:26.443 --> 00:04:31.105
One limit is worth remembering: the
standard covers federal employees only.

69
00:04:31.105 --> 00:04:34.770
Next, how those values
land in the directories.

70
00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:43.173
The crosswalk follows one design rule
above all: use a native Entra attribute

71
00:04:43.173 --> 00:04:47.608
wherever one exists, because other
Microsoft features already read them.

72
00:04:47.608 --> 00:04:52.930
Extensions come in only where a dynamic
membership rule can't read the native value,

73
00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.862
and that's a real gap:
rules can't see worker type,

74
00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:57.945
the org data block or the manager.

75
00:04:57.945 --> 00:05:01.595
So three extension attributes
carry codes for access:

76
00:05:01.595 --> 00:05:04.920
organization, worker type
and position category.

77
00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.090
They exist in both Active
Directory and Entra ID,

78
00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:13.320
so a rule works the same for hybrid and
cloud users through years of coexistence.

79
00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:16.032
And they hold codes, never display names.

80
00:05:16.032 --> 00:05:19.860
Next, the rest of the slots,
and who may write them.

81
00:05:23.610 --> 00:05:27.378
The other books need more slots,
so there's one program-wide

82
00:05:27.378 --> 00:05:31.460
map of all fifteen extension
attributes, and every page uses it.

83
00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:33.860
But the slide's real
point is on the right.

84
00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.442
A dynamic group hands the access decision

85
00:05:36.442 --> 00:05:39.097
to whoever can write
the attribute it reads.

86
00:05:39.097 --> 00:05:44.572
So each slot gets exactly one writer,
almost always the provisioning service, and

87
00:05:44.572 --> 00:05:47.350
every other write path, whether help desk,

88
00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:50.210
self-service or mail
tooling, gets removed.

89
00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:52.348
A write by anyone else is drift.

90
00:05:52.348 --> 00:05:54.198
That's the end of the data model.

91
00:05:54.198 --> 00:05:56.880
Module 2 is how the data moves.

92
00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:05.826
Module 2 is the provisioning pipeline:
how records travel from the HR platform,

93
00:06:05.826 --> 00:06:09.824
through middleware the agency
runs inside its own boundary,

94
00:06:09.824 --> 00:06:12.142
into Entra ID and Active Directory.

95
00:06:12.142 --> 00:06:14.004
We start with Federal HR 2.0,

96
00:06:14.004 --> 00:06:19.100
because its schedule decides when
the identity feed has to be ready.

97
00:06:22.850 --> 00:06:27.137
Federal HR 2.0 isn't the
project; it's the opening.

98
00:06:27.137 --> 00:06:31.168
One government-wide HR
platform, awarded in June 2026,

99
00:06:31.168 --> 00:06:37.213
with agencies moving in waves and the
older HR data systems eventually retired.

100
00:06:37.213 --> 00:06:40.150
The detail engineers need
is on the bottom row.

101
00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:43.958
The procurement does require
SCIM, but in one direction only:

102
00:06:43.958 --> 00:06:49.138
provisioning the HR platform's own users
from the government's identity provider.

103
00:06:49.138 --> 00:06:52.575
Nothing in it makes HR drive
the agency's directory.

104
00:06:52.575 --> 00:06:55.664
That reverse direction is
the agency's to design,

105
00:06:55.664 --> 00:06:59.062
and Microsoft documents how
for this exact platform.

106
00:06:59.062 --> 00:07:01.546
The window closes at the interconnection,

107
00:07:01.546 --> 00:07:04.526
around month five of
onboarding in the sample plan.

108
00:07:04.526 --> 00:07:06.880
Next, the pipeline itself.

109
00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:12.742
Here's the Microsoft realization.

110
00:07:12.742 --> 00:07:17.560
Middleware inside the agency's boundary
reads the HR events and posts them to a

111
00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:20.525
single Graph endpoint,
and Entra's provisioning

112
00:07:20.525 --> 00:07:23.193
service decides what to
do with each record.

113
00:07:23.193 --> 00:07:24.481
That split matters.

114
00:07:24.481 --> 00:07:29.391
The client doesn't decide whether to
create or update; the service does, and it

115
00:07:29.391 --> 00:07:34.380
reports results asynchronously, so the
client has to go back and read the logs.

116
00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:35.480
Why this pattern?

117
00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:39.617
Oracle's HR platform has no native
Entra connector, Microsoft's own

118
00:07:39.617 --> 00:07:44.205
guidance is this pattern, and one entry
point gives one provenance stream.

119
00:07:44.205 --> 00:07:48.480
If a native connector ships later,
it slots in as an optimization.

120
00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.320
Next, what the middleware actually does.

121
00:07:55.070 --> 00:07:59.974
On the source side, a full extract seeds
the population and event feeds carry the

122
00:07:59.974 --> 00:08:02.871
changes, and the feeds
have to start right after

123
00:08:02.871 --> 00:08:05.620
the initial load or
changes fall into the gap.

124
00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:08.720
The middleware then does
five things for every event.

125
00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.245
Two deserve emphasis.

126
00:08:10.245 --> 00:08:13.369
Sequencing, because a
mover and a leaver applied

127
00:08:13.369 --> 00:08:16.258
out of order is a correctness
bug, not a glitch.

128
00:08:16.258 --> 00:08:19.140
And provenance, written
before the call returns,

129
00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.670
because without that record
there's no verifiable outcome.

130
00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:26.103
One more rule: send the
complete record every time,

131
00:08:26.103 --> 00:08:28.782
or an empty field can clear a real value.

132
00:08:28.782 --> 00:08:32.210
Next, the contract that endpoint enforces.

133
00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:40.621
The endpoint has a contract worth
designing around: batches of up to fifty,

134
00:08:40.621 --> 00:08:44.595
a daily call cap that depends on
licensing, and a rate limit that

135
00:08:44.595 --> 00:08:48.798
two Microsoft pages state differently,
so pace to the stricter one.

136
00:08:48.798 --> 00:08:52.424
Give the client only the upload
and log-reading permissions,

137
00:08:52.424 --> 00:08:55.834
ideally the scoped version that
limits it to apps it owns.

138
00:08:55.834 --> 00:08:59.794
Three design rules sit underneath:
one provisioning app per source,

139
00:08:59.794 --> 00:09:03.965
no client secrets, and a pause switch
held by an operator rather than the

140
00:09:03.965 --> 00:09:08.773
middleware, so a bad batch can be stopped
before it disables thousands of accounts.

141
00:09:08.773 --> 00:09:11.250
Next, where the users land.

142
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:17.157
The same feed can land in two places,

143
00:09:17.157 --> 00:09:20.875
and which one changes as the
agency leaves Active Directory.

144
00:09:20.875 --> 00:09:26.203
While AD masters the account, users land
in AD through the provisioning agent,

145
00:09:26.203 --> 00:09:29.950
with their organizational
units (OUs) computed from HR

146
00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:33.863
data rather than picked by hand,
and then sync to the cloud.

147
00:09:33.863 --> 00:09:39.058
Once a population's accounts move to
the cloud, users land in Entra ID, where

148
00:09:39.058 --> 00:09:44.763
structure is just attributes and a
reorganization is a data change, not a project.

149
00:09:44.763 --> 00:09:50.355
The two programs converge population by
population: HR is the source for the person,

150
00:09:50.355 --> 00:09:55.462
Entra ID masters the account, and AD
shrinks to what applications still need.

151
00:09:55.462 --> 00:09:56.463
That's Part 1.

152
00:09:56.463 --> 00:09:58.790
Part 2 picks up the lifecycle.

153
00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:06.540
Module 3 is lifecycle,
credentials and access.

154
00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:11.064
With trusted data and a working
pipe, the HR event becomes the cause:

155
00:10:11.064 --> 00:10:15.753
nobody requests an account for a new
hire, because the hire creates it.

156
00:10:15.753 --> 00:10:18.555
We'll follow that event
through the lifecycle,

157
00:10:18.555 --> 00:10:22.760
through the PIV card and
certificates, and finally into access.

158
00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:30.698
For federal employees, the
trigger vocabulary already exists.

159
00:10:30.698 --> 00:10:34.147
Every personnel action carries
a nature-of-action code,

160
00:10:34.147 --> 00:10:38.497
and its family tells you the identity
event: appointments are joiners,

161
00:10:38.497 --> 00:10:42.172
separations are leavers, and
conversions keep the same key.

162
00:10:42.172 --> 00:10:45.152
The table is the book's
proposed interpretation,

163
00:10:45.152 --> 00:10:49.248
which HR and policy owners validate
before anything is automated.

164
00:10:49.248 --> 00:10:51.848
Three rules prevent the classic failures.

165
00:10:51.848 --> 00:10:56.459
Leave isn't a leaver, so a furlough
restricts an account rather than removing it.

166
00:10:56.459 --> 00:11:00.035
When two actions land
together, a separation wins.

167
00:11:00.035 --> 00:11:03.885
And a cancelled separation
re-enables access, with evidence.

168
00:11:03.885 --> 00:11:08.197
Contractors have no such codes;
their triggers are contract dates.

169
00:11:08.197 --> 00:11:10.780
Next, who acts on these events.

170
00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:16.569
Two services share the lifecycle,

171
00:11:16.569 --> 00:11:19.555
and mixing them up is the
most common design error.

172
00:11:19.555 --> 00:11:23.893
Provisioning creates and updates
accounts and turns them on or off.

173
00:11:23.893 --> 00:11:27.931
Lifecycle Workflows act on
accounts that already exist:

174
00:11:27.931 --> 00:11:31.454
the welcome, the access
packages, the leaver tasks.

175
00:11:31.454 --> 00:11:34.555
The schedule is the
engineering detail that bites.

176
00:11:34.555 --> 00:11:39.194
Workflows run every three hours by
default, on one tenant-wide setting,

177
00:11:39.194 --> 00:11:42.793
so that interval is part of
how fast a leaver is blocked.

178
00:11:42.793 --> 00:11:45.915
And a separation recorded
more than three days late

179
00:11:45.915 --> 00:11:49.255
misses the window entirely
and needs the on-demand path.

180
00:11:49.255 --> 00:11:51.355
Notice the gap at the bottom, too.

181
00:11:51.355 --> 00:11:53.520
That brings us to credentials.

182
00:11:57.270 --> 00:12:02.195
Here's the uncomfortable fact: disabling
an account doesn't revoke a certificate.

183
00:12:02.195 --> 00:12:06.672
The PIV card is bound to the Entra
user through an attribute holding the

184
00:12:06.672 --> 00:12:09.327
certificate's identifiers, and the strong,

185
00:12:09.327 --> 00:12:13.045
high-affinity bindings identify
exactly one certificate.

186
00:12:13.045 --> 00:12:18.170
So every reissue or renewal means the
card system has to rewrite the binding.

187
00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:20.870
Who can write it depends on
where the user is mastered.

188
00:12:20.870 --> 00:12:25.808
Today, for synced users, only Entra
Connect can, from Active Directory.

189
00:12:25.808 --> 00:12:29.207
Once the account moves to the
cloud, the design guidance is

190
00:12:29.207 --> 00:12:32.820
that the card system's issuance
event writes it through Graph.

191
00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:37.270
And the warning in red: whoever writes
that value decides who signs in.

192
00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:39.160
Next, revocation.

193
00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:44.773
Revocation has its own timing.

194
00:12:44.773 --> 00:12:48.001
Entra checks the issuing
authority's revocation list,

195
00:12:48.001 --> 00:12:51.659
but it caches that list and
doesn't support live status checks,

196
00:12:51.659 --> 00:12:56.035
so a revoked card stops working on the
authority's schedule, not instantly.

197
00:12:56.035 --> 00:13:00.572
That's why the account block and token
revocation matter: they close Entra now.

198
00:13:00.572 --> 00:13:02.272
Devices are the other trap.

199
00:13:02.272 --> 00:13:06.669
Intune revokes a device certificate
when you retire or wipe the device,

200
00:13:06.669 --> 00:13:09.798
but not when you delete the
user or pull the license.

201
00:13:09.798 --> 00:13:14.156
So the order is retire first and
delete last, and remove certificate

202
00:13:14.156 --> 00:13:18.748
assignments before revoking by hand,
or the device simply gets a new one.

203
00:13:18.748 --> 00:13:21.540
That order becomes the leaver sequence.

204
00:13:25.290 --> 00:13:29.102
The leaver sequence has one
rule at its heart: block first.

205
00:13:29.102 --> 00:13:31.337
At the effective separation time,

206
00:13:31.337 --> 00:13:35.727
disable the account and revoke
sessions, back to back, in that order.

207
00:13:35.727 --> 00:13:39.759
Everything else, the PIV card,
the devices and the access,

208
00:13:39.759 --> 00:13:44.578
runs after the block, in parallel,
and nothing is allowed to delay it.

209
00:13:44.578 --> 00:13:49.927
Each result is reconciled, and anything
incomplete escalates until it's confirmed.

210
00:13:49.927 --> 00:13:51.915
Deleting the account comes last,

211
00:13:51.915 --> 00:13:55.816
because deleting first strands
valid certificates on devices.

212
00:13:55.816 --> 00:13:59.276
In Lifecycle Workflows, the
steps with no built-in task

213
00:13:59.276 --> 00:14:02.890
go through one custom extension
that waits for an answer.

214
00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:05.252
Two clocks still run outside the block.

215
00:14:05.252 --> 00:14:06.920
Next, access.

216
00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:14.108
Once the attributes are trusted,
access follows from them.

217
00:14:14.108 --> 00:14:18.021
Dynamic groups compute membership
from the coded attributes,

218
00:14:18.021 --> 00:14:21.182
so nobody joins by hand and
nobody forgets to leave.

219
00:14:21.182 --> 00:14:26.392
Every access rule also excludes disabled
accounts, guests and agent identities,

220
00:14:26.392 --> 00:14:29.969
and runs in shadow before it's
attached to anything real.

221
00:14:29.969 --> 00:14:33.370
The right side matters just
as much: where rules stop.

222
00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:38.002
Privileged access uses assigned
groups and just-in-time activation,

223
00:14:38.002 --> 00:14:41.983
so HR decides who's eligible,
never who holds standing power.

224
00:14:41.983 --> 00:14:47.162
Dynamic administrative units (AUs) aren't
available in every government cloud,

225
00:14:47.162 --> 00:14:50.270
and break-glass accounts
depend on none of this.

226
00:14:50.270 --> 00:14:51.607
That's the lifecycle.

227
00:14:51.607 --> 00:14:54.220
Module 4 is how you prove it works.

228
00:14:57.970 --> 00:15:01.595
Module 4 is evidence,
ownership and the plan.

229
00:15:01.595 --> 00:15:04.066
An architecture that can't measure its own

230
00:15:04.066 --> 00:15:06.820
revocation time is taking
compliance on faith.

231
00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:11.317
So we'll cover what the event stream lets
you measure, who owns the data, the phased

232
00:15:11.317 --> 00:15:16.100
plan and its link to the program's foundation
gate, and the contractor question.

233
00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:23.763
Once events drive identity,
every interval becomes a number.

234
00:15:23.763 --> 00:15:28.332
The six metrics on the left measure things
a ticket-driven estate simply can't:

235
00:15:28.332 --> 00:15:32.688
how long from separation to block, from
separation to the last revocation,

236
00:15:32.688 --> 00:15:36.187
and how many accounts actually
tie back to a current worker.

237
00:15:36.187 --> 00:15:40.269
On the right are the drift classes:
each kind of difference between

238
00:15:40.269 --> 00:15:44.425
HR and the directory gets a detection
signal and a default severity.

239
00:15:44.425 --> 00:15:49.073
One expectation to set with leadership:
the first month brings a burst of

240
00:15:49.073 --> 00:15:54.263
high-severity findings, old leavers and
orphans the manual process never caught.

241
00:15:54.263 --> 00:15:57.337
That's the measurement working,
not the pipeline failing.

242
00:15:57.337 --> 00:15:59.700
Next, keeping the evidence.

243
00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.450
Evidence has a shelf-life problem.

244
00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:10.115
Most of what Entra ID records, from
provisioning logs to workflow history

245
00:16:10.115 --> 00:16:14.550
and audit events, is kept for thirty
days at best, and some of it for seven.

246
00:16:14.550 --> 00:16:17.353
Federal records schedules run far longer,

247
00:16:17.353 --> 00:16:22.638
and Entra's retention isn't retroactive:
whatever expires before export is gone.

248
00:16:22.638 --> 00:16:25.483
So route the logs out from
the first day of the pilot,

249
00:16:25.483 --> 00:16:30.088
keep them for the records schedule, and
keep personal data out of operational logs.

250
00:16:30.088 --> 00:16:34.388
The account is disposed of on schedule;
the evidence has to outlive it.

251
00:16:34.388 --> 00:16:38.120
Next, what all this evidence
does and doesn't prove.

252
00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:45.283
This architecture supports
controls; it doesn't close them.

253
00:16:45.283 --> 00:16:50.049
For account management, termination,
transfer, identifiers and retention,

254
00:16:50.049 --> 00:16:52.432
it supplies mechanisms and evidence,

255
00:16:52.432 --> 00:16:56.891
and the right column is what the agency
still owns: the defined periods,

256
00:16:56.891 --> 00:17:01.120
the procedures, the accounts outside
HR, and the records decisions.

257
00:17:01.120 --> 00:17:03.482
That's worth saying
plainly to an assessor.

258
00:17:03.482 --> 00:17:08.585
The book also asks for a clean separation
between what binds, like federal ICAM

259
00:17:08.585 --> 00:17:13.458
policy and the PIV standard, and what
only guides, like the lifecycle playbook.

260
00:17:13.458 --> 00:17:15.960
Now, the people behind the data.

261
00:17:19.710 --> 00:17:24.606
HR-driven identity is an organizational
change before it's a technical one,

262
00:17:24.606 --> 00:17:27.685
and most of the owners sit
outside the CIO's shop.

263
00:17:27.685 --> 00:17:29.897
The rule is one decider per row.

264
00:17:29.897 --> 00:17:34.354
The governance body settles who owns
each attribute and the person key,

265
00:17:34.354 --> 00:17:37.073
because those bind
several offices at once.

266
00:17:37.073 --> 00:17:39.611
HR owns the meaning of its own codes.

267
00:17:39.611 --> 00:17:45.347
Security owns leaver timing, and the
authorizing official accepts any residual risk.

268
00:17:45.347 --> 00:17:49.492
The failures on the right share one
root: in a ticket-driven estate,

269
00:17:49.492 --> 00:17:52.561
whoever can make a change
ends up owning the data.

270
00:17:52.561 --> 00:17:55.585
Privacy arriving at
go-live is the costliest.

271
00:17:55.585 --> 00:17:57.210
Next, the plan.

272
00:18:00.960 --> 00:18:05.610
The plan has seven phases, and each
gate is the next one's precondition.

273
00:18:05.610 --> 00:18:10.022
The thresholds are proposals the
agency approves, not federal rules.

274
00:18:10.022 --> 00:18:12.939
Phases one and two produce
nothing users can see,

275
00:18:12.939 --> 00:18:17.239
which is exactly why they get skipped,
and why estates end up explaining

276
00:18:17.239 --> 00:18:21.248
to an assessor how automation
deprovisioned a regional director.

277
00:18:21.248 --> 00:18:23.984
The panel ties this book
to the wider program.

278
00:18:23.984 --> 00:18:29.362
Its foundation gate, G1, needs HR-to-cloud
provisioning running for the foundation

279
00:18:29.362 --> 00:18:32.130
scope, with the attribute slots confirmed

280
00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:35.135
free and single-writer,
before rollout begins.

281
00:18:35.135 --> 00:18:39.097
That's how the program avoids an
HR connector breaking mid-rollout.

282
00:18:39.097 --> 00:18:41.870
Next, cutting a population over.

283
00:18:45.620 --> 00:18:50.560
A population is cut over when HR is
provably its only source of accounts,

284
00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:52.820
not when the pipeline first runs.

285
00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:57.716
Acceptance tests are written in business
language, so HR and security can read them,

286
00:18:57.716 --> 00:19:02.258
and anything that could leave a separated
person with access blocks sign-off.

287
00:19:02.258 --> 00:19:06.791
Then comes a dry run on production
data, cutover with rollback ready,

288
00:19:06.791 --> 00:19:10.920
and seven days of proof that every
change traces to an HR event.

289
00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:14.195
Only then does the ticket path
for creating accounts close.

290
00:19:14.195 --> 00:19:17.995
That's the visible end state:
the hire creates the account.

291
00:19:17.995 --> 00:19:21.250
Last, the population HR doesn't cover.

292
00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:27.119
Contractors are the open question,

293
00:19:27.119 --> 00:19:30.700
and the book frames it honestly
as a decision for the agency.

294
00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:34.050
They aren't in the HR platform's
standards or its waves,

295
00:19:34.050 --> 00:19:39.075
and today they usually live in spreadsheets
nobody is required to keep current.

296
00:19:39.075 --> 00:19:41.625
No single candidate source is complete.

297
00:19:41.625 --> 00:19:46.525
The contingent worker record is the best
fit but has no contract or vetting data;

298
00:19:46.525 --> 00:19:49.123
the card system only covers badged people;

299
00:19:49.123 --> 00:19:52.687
and a suitability register may
not know when the work ends.

300
00:19:52.687 --> 00:19:57.998
So give each fact one authority, hang the
record on an accountable federal sponsor,

301
00:19:57.998 --> 00:20:00.888
and end access at the
earliest end condition.

302
00:20:00.888 --> 00:20:02.088
Start read-only.

303
00:20:02.088 --> 00:20:04.610
Finally, what to decide first.

304
00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:12.285
Six decisions, each with an
owner, and the table shows who.

305
00:20:12.285 --> 00:20:13.385
Two stand out.

306
00:20:13.385 --> 00:20:17.010
The enterprise person key,
because everything joins

307
00:20:17.010 --> 00:20:20.223
on it and the HR cutover
is when a weak key breaks.

308
00:20:20.223 --> 00:20:23.667
And naming the identity feed
in the HR interconnection,

309
00:20:23.667 --> 00:20:27.935
because that window closes on the
program's schedule, not the agency's.

310
00:20:27.935 --> 00:20:31.935
The first step needs no new platform:
measure before you automate.

311
00:20:31.935 --> 00:20:35.138
Baseline the correlation
key and the orphans,

312
00:20:35.138 --> 00:20:38.510
fix errors at the source,
then build, leaver first.

313
00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:43.985
HR-driven identity makes accounts and access
consequences of the personnel record.

314
00:20:43.985 --> 00:20:47.610
The book has the detail,
with every claim footnoted.

315
00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:48.440
Thank you.
